    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d380">

                <pb facs="1257" n="1226" xml:id="pg1226"/>

                <head>380. Telegram From the Embassy in Bulgaria to the Department of State<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d380-f1"/></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#float-left">1985.</seg><dateline rendition="#float-right"
                            ><placeName>Sofia</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1985-05-28">May 28, 1985, 0829Z</date></dateline></opener>
                <!--<pb facs="d380-2" n="2" type="facsimile" xml:id="d380-2"/>-->
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>The Antonov Trial in Rome: Bulgarian Response.</item>
                </list>
                <list type="references">
                    <head>REF</head>
                    <item>Sofia 1962.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d380-f2"/></item>
                </list>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>Confidential—Entire text.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Bulgaria greeted the opening day of the Antonov trial in Rome, Monday May
                        27, with BTA (Bulgarian press agency) declaration and a flood of articles
                        declaring his innocence.</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>We assume FBIS will carry the full text of the BTA declaration<anchor
                            n="3" xml:id="d380-f3"/> which broke no new ground, but did say that
                        world public opinion recognized the “campaign” against Bulgaria and that
                        many publications rejected the “Bulgarian connection”, pointing instead
                        towards the involvement of “intelligence centers of NATO.”</item>
                    <label>4.</label>
                    <item>A report from Rome<anchor n="4" xml:id="d380-f4"/> also noted that the
                        interest of the United States in the trial was shown by the presence of four
                        observers from the US Embassy in Rome.</item>
                    <!--<pb facs="d380-3" n="3" type="facsimile" xml:id="d380-3"/>-->
                    <label>5.</label>
                    <item>Local press coverage of the trial May 27 was massive—lengthy radio
                        commentaries and a special program on prime time television.</item>
                </list>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Lake</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d380-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d380-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Foreign Policy File, D850372–0084.
                    Confidential; Priority. Sent for information to Ankara, Istanbul, Munich, Rome,
                    and the mission to NATO.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d380-f2" n="2" xml:id="d380-f2-content">
                <p>Telegram 1962 from Sofia, May 23, reported on Bulgarian counter-propaganda on the
                    eve of the Antonov trial. (Department of State, Central Foreign Policy File,
                    D850366–0971)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d380-f3" n="3" xml:id="d380-f3-content">
                <p>Not found.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d380-f4" n="4" xml:id="d380-f4-content">
                <p>Telegram 13428 from Rome, May 28. (Department of State, Central Foreign Policy
                    File, D850372–0717)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
